# The Problem
Godot's web export build an index.js that provides **stub imports** for the .NET runtime: each stub is a function that returns ```{ runtime_idx: N; }```. In a normal >NET WASM app, dotnet.runtime.js runs first and replaces those stubs with real implementations (HTTP, WebSocket, bindings, etc) before ```WebAssembly.instantiate*``` is called.

In this branch we were not calling dotnet.js and just ignoring the .NET implementation, so the stubs are never replaced and can only call the GodotAPI which is instantiated. 

There were some attempts at figuring out this initialization issue of dotnet.js, if you attempt to replace the stubs you get the initialization error because dotnet was not initialized even though technically you can try to get the stubs replaced. So I wondered this time, can we trick dotnet.runtime.js into thinking we did in fact initialize it and to replace our stubs. 

I will say I am not sure if this is the best way to do it or if we will run into other issues, as when I attempted the wider test of .NET implementations I get a signature mismatch error. And I really only wanted to spend a timebox amount of time on this so I can get back to my project before GDC happens. 

So my **Patch** which I would rather call than a "Fix" is 
	1. load ```dotnet.runtime.js``` before ```index.js``` 
	2. Replace the stubs in the imports object right before instantiation
	3. wire the runtime's INTERNAL/helpers to the same ```Module``` and memory that Godot's build uses.

